    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "055348cd_62736211",
        "filename": "src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/CarrierPrivilegesTracker.java",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 69,
      "author": {
        "id": 1124543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-03-16T22:31:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I know we\u0027re planning on listening to package changes later, but most of the time, this info will be mostly static. Eating the memory to represent 200 (arbitrary number) user-installed + system apps when this class is just waiting for a broadcast to poke it doesn\u0027t do us much good in terms of idle memory footprint. Especially considering that a PackageInfo effectively expands to represent the entire app\u0027s manifest.\n\nSIM_STATE_CHANGED is expected to be very uncommon, as is CARRIER_CONFIG_CHANGED. Effectively it\u0027s more or less safe to assume that each will get to a terminal state once and then do nothing else (LOADED + final CCC just slightly after that). Holding onto a giant 200-long list of PackageInfo for the lifetime of the boot session isn\u0027t doing us any favors if we never look at it after those first two broadcasts.\n\nOf course there will always be carriers like Fi where SSC and CCC are comparatively \"chatty\" due to switching (still on the order of 10s of minutes to hours, and typically only when moving non-trivial distances), but re-getting the package list on each broadcast is still probably net lower resource consumption.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 69,
        "startChar": 36,
        "endLine": 69,
        "endChar": 49
      },
      "revId": "f42c6fcc1f0f18704381374d68d180986808ee39",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1f41a298_7e3c638f",
        "filename": "src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/CarrierPrivilegesTracker.java",
